      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2013 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Chronic subdural hematoma is a common presentation to the neurosurgical practice that tends to occur among the elderly."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We identify a rare occurrence of a chronic subdural hematoma in a young and otherwise healthy female patient who denied a traumatic brain injury."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Upon reviewing the literature, we found only one reported case of a spontaneous nature in a young patient. The subtle presentation of headaches and absence of neurological deficit could be mistaken as migraine and result in a delay in obtaining imaging and necessary treatment. In this report, we present a 22-year-old patient who complained of persistent headaches and was neurologically intact on examination. Cranial computed tomography revealed the presence of a right-sided chronic subdural hematoma; this was followed by magnetic resonance imaging plus angiogram, which ruled out the presence of a vascular abnormality. A burr-hole craniostomy procedure was used that resulted in resolution of symptoms."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Without a heightened clinical suspicion, a similar presentation could be missed, resulting in delayed management."
          }
        ]
      },
